Using 13 TeV proton-proton collision data, specifically 1.7 inverse femtobarns of luminosity collected by the LHCb detector, the shape of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^+
u_{\mu}$ differential decay rate is determined as a function of hadron recoil. This rate is reconstructed via the $D_s^{-}$ decay sequence into $K^-K^+\pi^-$, following $D_s^{*-}$ decay into $D_s^{-}\gamma$. The resulting rate is fit with CLN and BGL form factor parametrisations to derive key parameters for both models. At the 1 sigma level, the total decay width for $B_s^0$ mesons is found to be 1.44 ps$^{-1}$ with the lifetime at 1.52 ps, and the CKM matrix element $|V_{cb}|$ is determined as 4.56 x 10$^{-2}$. Additionally, contributions to hadron recoil are identified and quantified, accounting for 14% of the total decay rate. This measurement enhances understanding of $B_s^0$ decay and contributes to estimating $|V_{cb}|$ with higher precision. Furthermore, it facilitates investigation of heavy meson effective theory and establishes reference for potential anomalies in other rare processes. This finding has been compared with other relevant processes, namely $B_d^0ightarrow D^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ and $B_s^0ightarrow J/\psi\phi$. Moreover, differences between $B_s^0$ and $B_d^0$ decay modes have been highlighted, alongside exploration of possible phenomena beyond the Standard Model, such as lepton flavour violation and heavy neutral leptons. This detection has reached limits for both of these phenomena, with the constraint on the Standard Model being the most stringent to date, given that it exceeds the 1 sigma level for all relevant processes, including $B_d^0ightarrow \mu^+\mu^-\mu^{+}
